How many types of containers? → FSLogix has 2 primary container types:

1) Profile containers → A profile container is a container as the data related to a user’s profile, which is directly stored in the VHD(x).
•	Its a full remote profile solution for non-persistent environments.
•	Re-directs the entire user profile to the remote location.
•	Its configuration defines how and where the profile is re-directed.
•	The entire profile except redirection.xml is included in the profile container.
•	Profile container includes all the benefits of office containers.
•	Profile container VHD(x) will contain the entire Windows profile for the user except for: (i) the temp folder location. 
•	(ii) the IE (Internet Explorer) crash folder location.
Note: Exclusions are done with a redirection.xml file.

2) ODFC Container → An ODFC Container is a container type, which is focused on storing profile content that is unique to Microsoft Office applications. Full form of ODFC is Office Data File Container.
Office container re-directs only areas of the profile that are specific to MS Office apps.
•	A default ODFC container includes: 
1.	Office Activation 
2.	 Outlook
3.	 Outlook personalization 
4.	SharePoint 
5.	 OneDrive 
6.	 Skype for Business (legacy support)
Note: ODFC Containers are an optional configuration.

Agent Components of FSLogix:-

1.	frx.exe → It is a command line utility. The utility is installed to c:\program files\fslogix\apps.
SERVICES:
2.	frxsvc.exe → Service that communicates with the command line tool, the drivers and monitors the rules folder for changes.
3.	frxccd.exe → Service responsible for handling cloud cache operations. Display name → Fslogix Cloud Caching Service
4.	Drivers:- i) frxdrv.sys → This driver is responsible for handling file system and registry requests to make hiding and redirection work. 
ii) frxdrvvt.sys → This driver provides the advanced re-direction capabilities for profile and ODFC containers.
iii) frxccd.sys → Responsible for handling cloud cache.

