FSLogix App Rules:-
→ What is FSLogix Application Rule Set?
 Rule set files are a collection of rules that show, hide, redirect, or customize specific aspects of the registry, file system, applications, or printers. 
These rule set files are created by the FSLogix Apps Rule Editor and are processed by FSLogix Apps Service (frxsvc).
Types of Rules:-
 App Rules are of 3 types:-
1.	Hiding rule.
2.	Redirection rule.
3.	Specify value rule.
Hiding rule:- A hiding rule hides specific items from one user or group of users. Hiding rules can apply to files, folders, registry keys, registry values, printers, or fonts.
Redirection rule:- Redirection rules allow IT administrators to redirect non-profile or other specific data into the user profile containers.
To Ensure FSLogix is available on subsequent sign-in regardless of which virtual machine they sign into.
Specify Value rules: At sign-in the specify value rules will set a registry value for a specific user or group of users.
NOTE: FSLogix Apps rule set assignment doesn’t support Microsoft Entra ID cloud-only accounts.
Application Rule sets are assigned to users, groups, and other entities.
Assignment types: You can assign rule sets to the following entities:
•	User
•	Group
•	Process
•	Network location (IP Address)
•	Computer
•	Directory Container (distinguished name)
•	Environment Variable

NOTE:
•	Newly created rules automatically have the Everyone group assigned with the Applies setting configured to NO.
•	Multiple assignments can be created in one Rule Set.
•	The Rule Set is applied from top to bottom. 
•	When you create a rule two files are created: rule(.frx) and assignment (.fxa) files.
•	You need to copy the rules to every vm where you want it applied.
•	Rules and assignment files are placed in % program files %\FSLlogix\aps\rules.

BASIC TROUBLESHOOTING for Profile Containers →
1.	Check local group membership (Exclude and Include Groups).
2.	Check the Registry Settings for “enabled” and VHD location or CCDLocation.
3.	Check to ensure the correct permissions are assigned to the user on the FileShare.
 (a) The VHD location should be accessible by the user (Read/Write).
4.	Check if a local profile doesn’t already exist for the user.
5.	Check if the user doesn’t have a disconnected session.
6.	 Check the Profile logs in %ProgramData%\FSLogix\Logs\Profile

BASIC troubleshooting for Office Container (ODFC):-
1.	Check local Group Membership (exclude and include Group).
2.	Check the Registry Setting “enabled” and VHD location on CCLocation.
3.	Check to ensure the correct permissions are assigned to the user on the file share.
 a) The VHD location should be accessible by the user (Read-write).
4.	Check Windows Search Service is running.
5.	Validate the Outlook GPO settings.
6.	Check the ODFC log files at %ProgramData%\FSLogix\Logs\ODFC

FSLogix DEPLOYMENT :-

→ On-Premise ←If configuring
1)On supported Windows machine, install FSLogix using FSLogix App Setup.
2) FSLogix can be configured in two ways - i) GPO ii) Registry
3) If using GPO to configure FSLogix, we need to add the FSLogix.admx and FSLogix.adml files to the %sysvol%\ Policy Definition and %sysvol%\ Policy Definition \en-us respectively. 
This will add the Policy template to the local Group Policy.
4) Create a File Share and share with the users (allow Read/Write).
5) Configure below GPOs:
•	Enabled: set to Enabled and select Enabled
•	VHD location: set to Enabled and specify the path of the file share (e.g. \\fileserver.domain.com\fileshare)
6) Set OPTIONAL GPOs such as:
•	Delete Local Profile when VHD should apply
•	Volume Type
•	Prevent login with Temp Profile
•	Prevent login with failure
•	Size in MB
7) Ensure that the user is member of the “FSLogix Profile Include Group” in the local user group.( By default Everyone is added). 
             It is recommended to add the Administrator to the “FSLogix Profile Exclude Group” for troubleshooting purpose.
8) Login to the configured Windows machine using the account added to the FSLogix include group.
9) User should have FSLogix profile created and loaded.

→ AVD – Entra-Joined :-
1.	On the session host, install the FSLogix App using the FSLogix App setup.
2.	FSLogix can be configured in two ways → GPO and Registry.
3.	If using GPO to configure FSLogix, we need to add the FSLogix.admx and FSLogix.adml files to the %sysvol%\PolicyDefinitions and %sysvol%\PolicyDefinitions\en-US respectively.
This will add the policy template to the local group policy.
4.	Create a storage account and a file share within the storage account.
 Using Access Control from the file share assign below RBAC role to the users:
Role: SMB Share Contributor
5. Configure below GPOs (Required):
•	Enabled: Set to Enabled and select Enabled
•	VHD location: Set to Enabled and specify the path of the file share (e.g. \\storageaccountname.file.core.windows.net\fileshare)
6. Set optional GPOs such as:
•	Delete local profile when VHD should apply
•	Volume type
•	Prevent login with temp profile
•	Prevent login with failure
•	Size in MB
7. Ensure that the user is member of the “FSLogix Profile Include Group” in the local user groups (By default Everyone is added).
It is recommended to add the Administrators to the “FSLogix Profile Exclude Group” for troubleshooting purpose.
8.	Login to the AVD using the Windows App.
9.	User should have FSLogix profile created and loaded.

Disk Compaction:
•	A = Total used space
•	B = Total Movable size (Calculated by DefragSvc)
•	C = A - B
Formula:
•	If C > = (A × 0.02) → Compaction will run
•	If C < (A × 0.02) → Compaction will not run

Cleanup Invalid Session:
When CleanupInvalidSessions is configured, FSLogix attempts to clean up the invalid sessions (the VHD(x) mounted for the user’s profile isn’t properly detached) and allows a successful sign-in when the user signs in on the next session.
•	PATH → HKLM\Software\FSLogix\Apps
•	Value Name → CleanupInvalidSessions
•	Value Type → DWORD
•	Default value → 0 (default)







